# Credentials for notarisation, as 1Password references. This file holds no
# secrets and is committed deliberately; `op run` resolves each op:// reference
# at call time.
#
#   op run --env-file .env.op -- ./release.sh
#
# None of this involves the App Store. Nothing is submitted for review — Apple
# just reuses its developer identity systems to authenticate notarisation.
#
# Two ways to authenticate; pick one and delete or comment out the other.
#
# 1. App-specific password (simpler). Create at appleid.apple.com ->
#    Sign-In and Security -> App-Specific Passwords. Then:
#
#      op item create --category=Login --title="Apple Notarization" \
#        --url=appleid.apple.com 'username=<your apple id>' \
#        'app password=<the generated password>' 'team id=<your team id>'
#
# AC_APPLE_ID=op://Private/Apple Notarization/username
# AC_APP_PASSWORD=op://Private/Apple Notarization/app password
AC_TEAM_ID=op://Private/Apple Notarization/team id
#
# 2. App Store Connect API key (better if this ever moves to CI, because it is
#    scoped and revocable on its own). appstoreconnect.apple.com -> Users and
#    Access -> Integrations. The .p8 downloads exactly once. Omit the issuer for
#    an Individual key; it is required for a Team key.
#
AC_API_KEY_ID=op://Private/Apple Notarization/key id
AC_API_ISSUER_ID=op://Private/Apple Notarization/issuer id
AC_API_KEY_P8=op://Private/Apple Notarization/private key
